{"timestamp": "2026-09-13T20:59:02.843988+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "logger": "openai._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'User-Agent': 'pydantic-ai/1.107.1'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-23a41654-d02c-4c88-98e7-e6e615cb58da', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You author one already-selected educational capability. Follow the\\nsupplied AuthoringDefinition and exact output schema. Preserve block identity, objective, scope,\\nlearner action, source ownership and assessment mode. Do not select another capability or redesign\\nthe teaching plan.\\n\\nPlanning instructions are not final content. For an activity, produce a meaningful prompt, task\\ndata, justified answer relationships and useful feedback as required by the contract. Do not infer\\ncorrectness from option positions, number/word positions, example ordering or arbitrary defaults.\\nDo not copy illustrative examples as unrelated lesson content.\\n\\nTreat approved items as authoritative content. Conversion must preserve their question and answer\\nmeaning. Report incompatibility through the defined error path instead of guessing. Use supported\\nteacher-review only when the work order policy permits it.\\n\\nReturn exactly the selected output schema. Do not emit sibling schemas, markdown wrappers, internal\\nplanning metadata or extra fields. If required information is absent, use the configured\\nmissing-input mechanism; do not create placeholder content to satisfy structure.\\n\\nDo not invent or overwrite knowledge/assessment policy fields. Effective policy is code-owned.\\n\\n## KNOWLEDGE POLICY\\nKnowledge policy: use supplied facts as the primary basis when present; do not describe model knowledge as approved factual material.\\n\\n## PACKAGE CAPABILITY INSTRUCTIONS\\nYou write LEARNER-FACING content for ONE ordinary document primitive.\\n\\nAllowed kinds: paragraph, heading, list, table, callout, figure.\\n(Figure: write caption + alt only. Asset generation uses the figure pipeline.)\\n\\nHard rules:\\n- Fill ONLY fields for the assigned kind. No unrelated fields.\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, or media objects.\\n- Do not author interactions; those use the interaction writer.\\n- The Teaching Plan brief is a WRITER INSTRUCTION, not student-facing text.\\n  Never copy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\"Row 1\"), generic headers (\"Item\"/\"Detail\"),\\n  or \"Content pending.\"\\n\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\n- paragraph \u2192 { \"text\": string }\\n- heading \u2192 { \"text\": string, \"level\": 1|2|3 }\\n- list \u2192 { \"ordered\": boolean, \"items\": [string, ...] }\\n- table \u2192 { \"headers\": [string, ...], \"rows\": [[string, ...], ...], \"caption\": string }\\n- callout \u2192 { \"tone\": \"note\"|\"warning\"|\"tip\"|\"important\", \"title\": string, \"body\": string }\\n- figure \u2192 { \"caption\": string, \"alt\": string }\\n\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\n\\n\\n## AUTHORING DEFINITION\\n{\\n  \"assessment\": {},\\n  \"capability_id\": \"document-writer:paragraph\",\\n  \"converter_ref\": null,\\n  \"definition_hash\": \"c24bc83c1b6259ec1b3c6c18b2e06a94499913121902ea0dcab3b68c3ffafde1:paragraph\",\\n  \"instructions\": \"You write LEARNER-FACING content for ONE ordinary document primitive.\\\\n\\\\nAllowed kinds: paragraph, heading, list, table, callout, figure.\\\\n(Figure: write caption + alt only. Asset generation uses the figure pipeline.)\\\\n\\\\nHard rules:\\\\n- Fill ONLY fields for the assigned kind. No unrelated fields.\\\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, or media objects.\\\\n- Do not author interactions; those use the interaction writer.\\\\n- The Teaching Plan brief is a WRITER INSTRUCTION, not student-facing text.\\\\n  Never copy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\\\\\"Row 1\\\\\"), generic headers (\\\\\"Item\\\\\"/\\\\\"Detail\\\\\"),\\\\n  or \\\\\"Content pending.\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\\\n- paragraph \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string }\\\\n- heading \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string, \\\\\"level\\\\\": 1|2|3 }\\\\n- list \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"ordered\\\\\": boolean, \\\\\"items\\\\\": [string, ...] }\\\\n- table \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"headers\\\\\": [string, ...], \\\\\"rows\\\\\": [[string, ...], ...], \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string }\\\\n- callout \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"tone\\\\\": \\\\\"note\\\\\"|\\\\\"warning\\\\\"|\\\\\"tip\\\\\"|\\\\\"important\\\\\", \\\\\"title\\\\\": string, \\\\\"body\\\\\": string }\\\\n- figure \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string, \\\\\"alt\\\\\": string }\\\\n\\\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\\\n\",\\n  \"knowledge\": {},\\n  \"modes\": [\\n    \"generate\"\\n  ],\\n  \"native_path\": \"document\",\\n  \"payload_schema\": {\\n    \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n    \"properties\": {\\n      \"kind\": {\\n        \"const\": \"paragraph\"\\n      },\\n      \"text\": {\\n        \"minLength\": 1,\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \"required\": [\\n      \"kind\",\\n      \"text\"\\n    ],\\n    \"type\": \"object\"\\n  },\\n  \"required_inputs\": [\\n    \"kind\",\\n    \"brief\",\\n    \"teaching_block\"\\n  ],\\n  \"validator_refs\": [\\n    \"document.writer_schema\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n## SCOPED REQUEST\\n{\\n  \"inputs\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"plants have roots, stems and leaves\",\\n      \"living things need food to grow\",\\n      \"Plants are living things that can produce their own food\",\\n      \"Plants are called producers\",\\n      \"Use one concrete classroom example.\",\\n      \"Explain that plants make their own food (are producers)\",\\n      \"Identify leaves as the site of photosynthesis and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance\",\\n      \"Describe photosynthesis as using light, carbon dioxide, and water to produce food and oxygen\",\\n      \"Explain why light is essential for photosynthesis\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Define producer for a plant: a producer is a living thing that makes its own food. State plainly that the windowsill plant is a producer, so its food is made by the plant itself and is not something delivered to it from outside. Keep the definition to this single idea and do not yet describe how the plant makes the food or what role sunlight plays.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food\",\\n      \"subject\": \"Science\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the classroom plant on the windowsill beside the cup of water and asks learners to commit to a guess about where its food comes from, so a personal wrong model is on the table. The explanation then fixes the category term \\\\u2014 the plant is a producer, a living thing that makes its own food \\\\u2014 and separates what the plant takes in through its roots from what it makes. The contrast section puts the producer idea against the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings on the same plant so the boundary is visible, and the close checks whether the learner now recognizes the plant, not the soil or the water, as the maker of the food.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Signal the commitment prompt clearly, since the learner\\'s initial guess must be on the table before the explanation is introduced; the interaction itself is authored separately.\",\\n        \"role\": \"elicit-initial-belief\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"heading\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Fix the category term \\'producer\\' as the section\\'s single idea so the definition that follows is easy to locate and reference.\",\\n        \"role\": \"define\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Build the separation on the same plant: roots draw in soil materials and water as raw material, while the food itself is made by the plant, keeping the plant as the maker.\",\\n        \"role\": \"explain\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"list\",\\n        \"reason\": \"A short two-part list makes the intake-versus-made distinction visually crisp so learners can hold the boundary rather than blending the two categories.\",\\n        \"role\": \"explain\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"reason\": \"State the definition \\\\u2014 a producer is a living thing that makes its own food \\\\u2014 and apply it plainly to the windowsill plant, without drifting into mechanism or sunlight.\",\\n    \"role\": \"define\",\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Define producer for a plant: a producer is a living thing that makes its own food. State plainly that the windowsill plant is a producer, so its food is made by the plant itself and is not something delivered to it from outside. Keep the definition to this single idea and do not yet describe how the plant makes the food or what role sunlight plays.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"The objective\\'s verb is \\'recognize\\' a category, not explain a mechanism, so the slot needs the category term \\'producer\\' fixed precisely before any contrast with soil or water can mean anything.\",\\n      \"id\": \"explain-b1\",\\n      \"intent\": \"define\"\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": []\\n  },\\n  \"mode\": \"generate\",\\n  \"scoped_request\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"plants have roots, stems and leaves\",\\n      \"living things need food to grow\",\\n      \"Plants are living things that can produce their own food\",\\n      \"Plants are called producers\",\\n      \"Use one concrete classroom example.\",\\n      \"Explain that plants make their own food (are producers)\",\\n      \"Identify leaves as the site of photosynthesis and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance\",\\n      \"Describe photosynthesis as using light, carbon dioxide, and water to produce food and oxygen\",\\n      \"Explain why light is essential for photosynthesis\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Define producer for a plant: a producer is a living thing that makes its own food. State plainly that the windowsill plant is a producer, so its food is made by the plant itself and is not something delivered to it from outside. Keep the definition to this single idea and do not yet describe how the plant makes the food or what role sunlight plays.\",\\n    \"evidence\": \"The objective\\'s verb is \\'recognize\\' a category, not explain a mechanism, so the slot needs the category term \\'producer\\' fixed precisely before any contrast with soil or water can mean anything.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food\",\\n      \"subject\": \"Science\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the classroom plant on the windowsill beside the cup of water and asks learners to commit to a guess about where its food comes from, so a personal wrong model is on the table. The explanation then fixes the category term \\\\u2014 the plant is a producer, a living thing that makes its own food \\\\u2014 and separates what the plant takes in through its roots from what it makes. The contrast section puts the producer idea against the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings on the same plant so the boundary is visible, and the close checks whether the learner now recognizes the plant, not the soil or the water, as the maker of the food.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Signal the commitment prompt clearly, since the learner\\'s initial guess must be on the table before the explanation is introduced; the interaction itself is authored separately.\",\\n        \"role\": \"elicit-initial-belief\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"heading\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Fix the category term \\'producer\\' as the section\\'s single idea so the definition that follows is easy to locate and reference.\",\\n        \"role\": \"define\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Build the separation on the same plant: roots draw in soil materials and water as raw material, while the food itself is made by the plant, keeping the plant as the maker.\",\\n        \"role\": \"explain\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"list\",\\n        \"reason\": \"A short two-part list makes the intake-versus-made distinction visually crisp so learners can hold the boundary rather than blending the two categories.\",\\n        \"role\": \"explain\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"objective\": \"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food\",\\n    \"reason\": \"State the definition \\\\u2014 a producer is a living thing that makes its own food \\\\u2014 and apply it plainly to the windowsill plant, without drifting into mechanism or sunlight.\",\\n    \"role\": \"define\",\\n    \"stage\": \"document_writing\",\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Define producer for a plant: a producer is a living thing that makes its own food. State plainly that the windowsill plant is a producer, so its food is made by the plant itself and is not something delivered to it from outside. Keep the definition to this single idea and do not yet describe how the plant makes the food or what role sunlight plays.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"The objective\\'s verb is \\'recognize\\' a category, not explain a mechanism, so the slot needs the category term \\'producer\\' fixed precisely before any contrast with soil or water can mean anything.\",\\n      \"id\": \"explain-b1\",\\n      \"intent\": \"define\"\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": []\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nReturn JSON only for this capability payload.'}, {'role': 'system', 'content': 'Return only valid JSON matching this schema.\\nDo not call tools.\\n{\"properties\": {\"response\": {\"additionalProperties\": true, \"type\": \"object\"}}, \"required\": [\"response\"], \"type\": \"object\", \"title\": \"dict\"}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Return JSON only for the selected capability payload.'}], 'model': 'deepseek-flash', 'max_completion_tokens': 8000, 'stream': False}, 'extra_json': {'thinking': {'type': 'disabled'}}}"}
